{"id": 1610, "stem": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-text\"><span><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-100\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">A</mark> 1-day-old girl born to a 40-year-old woman is evaluated in the newborn nursery.\u00a0 The pregnancy and delivery were uncomplicated.\u00a0 Physical examination shows mid-face hypoplasia with a flat nasal bridge, up-slanting palpebral fissures, a small mouth, and a single palmar crease bilaterally.\u00a0 Echocardiography shows an atrioventricular septal defect.\u00a0 Which of the following genetic abnormalities could account for this patient's condition?\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c</p></span></div>", "options": [{"label": "A", "text": "Aberrant genomic imprinting"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Mosaicism"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Partial deletion"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Trinucleotide repeat expansion"}, {"label": "E", "text": "Uniparental disomy"}], "correctAnswer": "B", "explanation": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-explanation\"><span class=\"\"><div id=\"div_t80975\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><div class=\"group/triggerable\" data-is-open=\"false\"><div class=\"group/draggable\" data-is-draggable=\"true\" data-is-dragging=\"false\"><div class=\"group/resizable relative\" data-is-maximized=\"false\" data-is-resizable=\"true\" data-is-resizing=\"false\"><div class=\"relative max-w-full\"><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute left-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-r from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute right-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-l from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div class=\"max-w-full overflow-x-auto [&amp;_td]:min-w-[8rem] [&amp;_th]:min-w-[8rem]\"><table class=\"border bg-transparent dark:text-white border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white hover:bg-transparent warm:hover:bg-transparent dark:hover:bg-transparent cursor-default mt-2 mb-3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tbody style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" colspan=\"3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-50\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">I</mark>nheritance of Down syndrome</strong></p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Mechanism</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Pathogenesis</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Recurrence risk</strong></p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Meiotic nondisjunction </strong>(~95%)</p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Error during gamete formation (usually maternal meiosis I)</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Extra copy of chromosome 21 in every cell</li></ul></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Based on maternal age</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Unbalanced Robertsonian translocation</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">De novo or inherited from parent with balanced translocation</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Extra copy of 21q fused to another chromosome (usually 14)</li></ul></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">High if parent carries balanced translocation</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Mosaicism</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Postzygotic error (eg, mitotic nondisjunction)</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Some (not all) cells have extra copy of chromosome 21</li></ul></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Similar to normal population</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></div></div></div><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">This newborn has characteristic features of <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-wdv63wbb9t3ovrj6fcbfla9u\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-wdv63wbb9t3ovrj6fcbfla9u\">Down syndrome</a> (DS), which results from <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">increased gene dosage</strong> due to an <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">extra copy of chromosome 21</strong>.\u00a0 Three cytogenetic abnormalities can cause DS:</p><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start inline\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-vw26ehwwgcjgbynv46soxw8r\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-vw26ehwwgcjgbynv46soxw8r\">Meiotic nondisjunction</a> causes nearly 95% of cases.\u00a0 Failure of homologous chromosomes (meiosis I) or sister chromatids (meiosis II) to separate produces gametes with 2 copies of chromosome 21 or none.\u00a0 When a gamete with 2 copies is fertilized by a normal gamete, every cell of the resulting embryo has trisomy 21.\u00a0 Most cases arise in maternal meiosis I; risk rises with maternal age because oocytes remain arrested in prophase I from fetal life until ovulation, during which cohesin (the protein complex holding paired chromosomes together) gradually deteriorates, increasing segregation errors.</p></li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start inline\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Unbalanced Robertsonian translocation</strong> is less common.\u00a0 The long arms of 2 acrocentric chromosomes (usually 14 and 21) fuse, and the short arms are lost.\u00a0 Affected individuals have 46 chromosomes, including 2 normal copies of chromosome 21 and a fused chromosome carrying a third copy of 21q.\u00a0 Cases inherited from a parent with a balanced translocation carry a high recurrence risk.</p></li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start inline\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Mosaicism</strong> is also uncommon.\u00a0 Postzygotic mitotic nondisjunction can generate a trisomic cell line within an initially normal embryo.\u00a0 Alternatively, loss of the extra chromosome 21 from some cells of a trisomic embryo (trisomy rescue) can generate a normal cell line.\u00a0 The resulting mixture of normal and trisomic cells produces variable clinical severity, influenced by the proportion and tissue distribution of trisomic cells.</p></li></ul><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">Because these mechanisms cannot be reliably distinguished clinically, karyotyping is required to identify the cause.\u00a0 Of the listed options, only mosaicism produces a <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">third copy</strong> of chromosome 21 in at least some cells.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice A)</strong>\u00a0 Genomic imprinting is the normal epigenetic silencing (eg, by DNA methylation) of certain genes based on parent of origin.\u00a0 Aberrant imprinting (eg, Prader-Willi and Angelman syndromes) alters expression of these genes without adding genetic material.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice C)</strong>\u00a0 Partial deletions (eg, cri du chat syndrome [5p deletion]) reduce gene dosage, whereas DS results from increased dosage.\u00a0 The short arms lost in Robertsonian translocations contain mainly redundant ribosomal RNA genes, so their loss is clinically insignificant.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice D)</strong>\u00a0 Trinucleotide repeat expansion can silence a gene (eg, fragile X syndrome) or produce an abnormal protein (eg, Huntington disease) but does not change chromosome number.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice E)</strong>\u00a0 Uniparental disomy (UPD) is inheritance of both copies of a chromosome from 1 parent; chromosome number and gene dosage are normal.\u00a0 UPD causes disease mainly by altering expression of imprinted genes.\u00a0 A child with 2 copies from one parent plus 1 from the other has trisomy, not UPD.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Educational objective:</strong>Common findings in Down syndrome include cognitive impairment, facial dysmorphism, and cardiac defects.\u00a0 Approximately 95% of cases result from meiotic nondisjunction (usually maternal).\u00a0 Less common causes are unbalanced Robertsonian translocation and mosaicism (normal and trisomic cell lines due to a postzygotic error).\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c</p></span></div>", "educationalObjective": "Common findings in Down syndrome include cognitive impairment, facial dysmorphism, and cardiac defects.  Approximately 95% of cases result from meiotic nondisjunction (usually maternal).  Less common causes are unbalanced Robertsonian translocation and mosaicism (normal and trisomic cell lines due to a postzygotic error).\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c", "subject": "Genetics", "system": "Genetics (General Principles)", "topic": "Aneuploidy", "category": "Clinical genetics", "peerCorrect": 44.0}